ssues for a human (never for an agent) and needs a TruffleHog binary (AGPL-3.0; it is installed by the role's Dockerfile or taken from the host, and is not in the ard image). To opt out, delete `.ard/scanners/trufflehog/` and its `scanners/trufflehog` line under `overrides:` in `.ard/config.yaml` before merging.\\n\")\n./internal/executor/local/docker.go:120:\tout, err := l.docker(bctx, \"build\", \"-t\", tag, \"-f\", j.Dockerfile, j.RoleDir)\n./internal/executor/local/hostwarn.go:18:const hostHint = \"add a Dockerfile to the role directory, or set ARD_AGENT_IMAGE, to run it under Docker\"\n./internal/executor/local/hostwarn.go:33:// no Dockerfile. kind and name come from config.Role; this package does not\n./internal/executor/local/hostwarn.go:43:\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"WARNING: %s role %q reads untrusted input but has no Dockerfile and ARD_AGENT_IMAGE is not set, so its jobs run as a host subprocess \"+\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:27:\t// runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no Dockerfile. A\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:28:\t// role's own Dockerfile wins. \"\" = such jobs run on the host as before.\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:151:// Dockerfile role, ensures the image. It starts no process.\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:165:\tuseDocker := j.Dockerfile != \"\"\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:172:\t\tif j.Dockerfile != \"\" {\n./internal/executor/local/local.go:173:\t\t\t// A role's own Dockerfile wins over the default image.\n./internal/executor/validate.go:53:\tfor name, p := range map[string]string{\"dockerfile\": j.Dockerfile} {\n./internal/executor/kube/kube.go:144:\tif j.Dockerfile != \"\" {\ncommit 8510429fb34fc2c7490771e2049dcf4b03b79af5\nAuthor: Al Brown <al@albrown.com>\nDate:   Thu Nov 26 09:32:35 2026 +0100\n\n    chart: in-cluster role image builder namespace and push credential (#448) (#450)\n    \n    With roleImages.enabled and executor k8s, ard-serve creates a build\n    namespace for rootless BuildKit: default-deny network policies with DNS and\n    internet egress, quota, limit range, the ard-build ServiceAccount, a Role for\n    the controller and an admission policy that admits only the pinned builder\n    pod shape. The ghcr push credential is a dockerconfigjson Secret in that\n    namespace, optionally from an ExternalSecret, mounted only by builder pods.\n    The controller gets the build settings as ARD_K8S_* env for #449.\n    \n    Off by default; the default and k8s renders are unchanged.\n\n deploy/charts/ard-serve/templates/_helpers.tpl     |  49 +++++\n .../ard-serve/templates/build-namespace.yaml       | 240 +++++++++++++++++++++\n deploy/charts/ard-serve/templates/deployment.yaml  |   9 +\n .../ard-serve/templates/externalsecret-push.yaml   |  43 ++++\n deploy/charts/ard-serve/values.yaml                |  39 ++++\n deploy/test-charts.sh                              |  86 ++++++++\n docs/proposal.md                                   |   3 +-\n docs/runbooks/role-images.md                       | 109 ++++++++++\n 8 files changed, 577 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)\n379:3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms. The aid job is read-only, has no `Env` and no `Fixed` entries (no model credential, no GitHub token), runs with `--network none` under Docker (a role `Dockerfile` or `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`; known CVEs stay with the `cve` scanner, so the aid never queries vulnerability databases), and has its own timeout of 5 minutes (shorter if the role's is), counted inside the review job's timeout: the agent gets what is left, at least one minute. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/changed.txt` (one path per line, `.ard/<issue#>/` left out) and `diff.patch`. Its output is JSONL on stdout, one object per result with exactly `tool`, `rule`, `file`, `line`, `severity` (`critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`) and `message`, capped at 256 KiB (more is a failure). ard validates it: a line that is not JSON, has an unknown key, a bad tool, rule, severity or line, or a path that is absolute, has `..` or is not in `changed.txt` is dropped and counted; a message is redacted, cut at 1 KiB and secret-scanned like a log, and a line that still trips the scanner is dropped. Matched text is never kept: the aid must print rule ids and positions only. The result is written to `.ard-input/<issue#>/aid.jsonl` and is the first of the agent's untrusted inputs; the starter prompt reads it first and runs the tools itself only when the file is absent. **Failure:** a failed, timed-out or invalid aid (any dropped line counts as invalid) never stops the review. The input says the aid failed and why, and close-out turns an `approve` into `changes` with a note. **Blocking:** a `gitleaks` result on a line the PR added becomes a blocking `high` finding with a `[secret]` message that ard adds to the review in code (carrying the rule id only); the agent cannot drop it, and an `approve` becomes `changes`. gosec and semgrep results stay advisory. Both rules apply only to roles that have an `aid`. **Host mode:** with no role `Dockerfile` and no `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`, the aid runs on the host, as ard-owned code that reads but never executes PR code; its environment holds no credential, but nothing enforces its network, and it runs as the controller's uid without a sandbox: the residual risk of #84 applies to it too. The starter `security-review/aid` pins gosec, gitleaks and semgrep (vendored local rules, `--metrics=off`, `--disable-nosem`) in the role's `Dockerfile`, built on the default ard-agent image (k8s role images stay with #245). Then **run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\n466:- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess (or, with `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE` set, a role that reads untrusted input runs in that image) and under `executor: k8s` every role runs the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters) and a role `Dockerfile` is ignored unless role images are on (`roleImages.enabled`, #448; ard serve builds them from #449, see Delivery: Role images). The starter scanners `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint` and `missing-tests` use the tools in the public image; `trufflehog` roles stay off under k8s, and `ard config check` will warn (planned, later part). A role Dockerfile should start `FROM` the local `ard-agent` image by digest or ID. A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n584:- **Role images ([#245](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/245)).** Owner decisions: role `Dockerfile`s are built **in the cluster** when a job needs one, not in CI; the images are **public** on ghcr as `<roleImages.repository>/<type>-<name>` (default `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/<type>-<name>`), so job pods pull them with no `imagePullSecrets`; a role image built on the agent image starts `FROM ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:latest`, so `ard-build` must also move the agent image's `:latest` tag. The chart side (#448, off by default): with `roleImages.enabled` and executor k8s, `deploy/charts/ard-serve` creates the build namespace `ard-build-<instance>`, apart from the jobs namespace because builder pods hold the push credential. Rootless BuildKit (`roleImages.builder.image`, pinned by digest) needs seccomp and AppArmor Unconfined, which Pod Security restricted and baseline forbid, so that namespace enforces privileged and warns and audits at baseline; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy admits only the builder shape instead: the `ard-build` ServiceAccount with no token, exactly one `buildkit` container running that exact image as `roleImages.builder.uid`, not privileged and adding no capabilities, no host namespaces, image pull secrets or envFrom, and Secret volumes limited to the push credential and the pod's own Secret. Builder pods reach DNS and the internet minus the private ranges, and nothing reaches them. The push credential is a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret, optionally built by an ExternalSecret from one item's `username` and `token` (a classic PAT with `write:packages`; ghcr refuses App and fine-grained tokens). The controller gets the build namespace, builder image and uid, role image repository and the push Secret's name as `ARD_K8S_BUILD_NAMESPACE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_IMAGE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_UID`, `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY` and `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_PUSH_SECRET`, never the credential, and a Role in the build namespace like the jobs one. **Residual risks:** the controller's token can create builder pods that mount the push credential, so a stolen controller token can push to the role image packages (the admission policy limits it to the pinned BuildKit image, not to a given build); and with a `:latest` base, a role image tagged by its role directory keeps an older agent base until the role changes. Each package must be made public once after its first push, and AGPL roles (`trufflehog`) stay off under k8s so they are never published. Runbook: [docs/runbooks/role-images.md](runbooks/role-images.md).\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\ndigest\ndocscheck\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\nimagescan\ninitcmd\nintake\njanitor\njobexec\njobpod\njobpost\njobpre\nlabels\nlimits\nmerger\nmodelauth\npool\nprgate\nprwatch\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nreviewfix\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nsetupcmd\nsnapshot\nstate\ntarball\ntriage\nui\nversioncheck\nworker\nwtlock","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2f535f0b-91e2-4cd6-a20e-142f0e47e2e5","uuid":"adcb6840-1733-498f-99f2-e1fcde2701fe","timestamp":"2026-11-26T08:33:47.124Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"./internal/servecmd/serve.go:411:\t// AgentImage is the default agent image (ARD_AGENT_IMAGE); \"\" = host mode for roles without a Dockerfile.\n./internal/config/render.go:91:\tDockerfile string   `yaml:\"dockerfile,omitempty\"`\n./internal/config/render.go:176:\t\t\tDockerfile:      role.Dockerfile,\n./internal/config/render.go:213:\tcase role.Dockerfile == \"\" && r.Executor != \"k8s\":\n./internal/config/types.go:130:\tDockerfile string   // Dir + \"/Dockerfile\"; \"\" => default ard-agent image\n./internal/config/types.go:207:\t// Ref = SHA and resolve Role.Dir, Role.Dockerfile and Role.PromptPath\n./internal/config/types.go:267:// Dockerfile, so the local executor runs them as a host subprocess under the\n./internal/config/types.go:274:// Dockerfile, or the default agent image), so none runs on the host.\n./internal/config/types.go:281:\t\tif role.ReadsUntrusted() && role.Dockerfile == \"\" {\n./internal/config/discover.go:26://   - an optional \"Dockerfile\" names the role image;\n./internal/config/discover.go:104:\tif _, has